    {
      "id": "e574e4f9-cb6a-40d7-85b6-038c7496d2b3",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Great question 👍\nThis code is **teaching static nested classes and comparators** using a very realistic example: *sorting employees in different ways*. I’ll walk you through it step-by-step, **line by line**, and also explain **why this design is useful in real projects**.\n\n---\n\n## Big Picture (What is this code demonstrating?)\n\n1. **Static nested class** (`Employee.EmployeeComparator`)\n2. **Comparator pattern** for sorting\n3. **Encapsulation** – sorting logic lives *inside* the `Employee` domain\n4. **Runtime sorting strategy** (sort by name or yearStarted)\n5. **Comparator chaining** (`reversed()`)\n\n---\n\n## 1️⃣ Main.java – How everything is used\n\n```java\nList<Employee> employees = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\n    new Employee(10001, \"Ralph\", 2015),\n    new Employee(10005, \"Carole\", 2021),\n    new Employee(10022, \"Jane\", 2013),\n    new Employee(13151, \"Laura\", 2020),\n    new Employee(10050, \"Jim\", 2018)\n));\n```\n\nYou create a list of `Employee` objects.\n\nEach employee has:\n\n* `employeeId`\n* `name`\n* `yearStarted`\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 Sorting using the **static nested class**\n\n```java\nemployees.sort(\n    new Employee.EmployeeComparator<>(\"yearStarted\").reversed()\n);\n```\n\nLet’s decode this carefully:\n\n| Part                          | Meaning                     |\n| ----------------------------- | --------------------------- |\n| `Employee.EmployeeComparator` | Static nested class         |\n| `\"yearStarted\"`               | Sorting strategy            |\n| `sort(...)`                   | Uses `Comparator<Employee>` |\n| `reversed()`                  | Reverse order               |\n\n➡️ This sorts employees **by yearStarted in descending order**\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 Output loop\n\n```java\nfor (Employee e : employees) {\n    System.out.println(e);\n}\n```\n\nUses `Employee.toString()` to print nicely formatted output.\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ Employee.java – The core design\n\n### 🔹 Fields (state)\n\n```java\nprivate int employeeId;\nprivate String name;\nprivate int yearStarted;\n```\n\nBasic domain data.\n\n---\n\n## 3️⃣ Static Nested Class – ⭐ KEY CONCEPT\n\n```java\npublic static class EmployeeComparator<T extends Employee>\n        implements Comparator<Employee> {\n```\n\n### What does **static nested class** mean?\n\n| Feature  | Explanation                                  |\n| -------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `static` | Does NOT depend on an instance of `Employee` |\n| Nested   | Logically grouped with `Employee`            |\n| Access   | Can access `Employee`’s private fields       |\n\n➡️ **Design idea:**\n\n> “This comparator exists *only* to compare Employees, so keep it inside Employee.”\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 Constructor Overloading\n\n```java\npublic EmployeeComparator() {\n    this(\"name\");\n}\n```\n\nDefault sort = **by name**\n\n```java\npublic EmployeeComparator(String sortType) {\n    this.sortType = sortType;\n}\n```\n\nDynamic sort behavior decided at runtime.\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 compare() method (core logic)\n\n```java\n@Override\npublic int compare(Employee o1, Employee o2) {\n\n    if (sortType.equalsIgnoreCase(\"yearStarted\")) {\n        return o1.yearStarted - o2.yearStarted;\n    }\n\n    return o1.name.compareTo(o2.name);\n}\n```\n\n#### How comparison works\n\n| Condition       | Result       |\n| --------------- | ------------ |\n| `\"yearStarted\"` | Sort by year |\n| anything else   | Sort by name |\n\nThis is **strategy-based sorting**.\n\n---\n\n## 4️⃣ Why `static` is important here\n\nIf this were **non-static**:\n\n```java\npublic class EmployeeComparator { ... }\n```\n\nYou’d need an `Employee` object to create a comparator ❌\n\nBut since it’s `static`:\n\n```java\nnew Employee.EmployeeComparator<>(\"yearStarted\");\n```\n\n✔️ No `Employee` instance required\n✔️ Cleaner and safer design\n\n---\n\n## 5️⃣ Comparator chaining – `.reversed()`\n\n```java\nemployees.sort(\n    new Employee.EmployeeComparator<>(\"yearStarted\").reversed()\n);\n```\n\n* `Comparator` has **default methods**\n* `reversed()` flips comparison result\n\n➡️ Without changing comparator code, you get descending order\n\n---\n\n## 6️⃣ EmployeeComparator.java (separate file)\n\n```java\npublic class EmployeeComparator<T extends Employee>\n        implements Comparator<Employee> {\n```\n\nThis is the **older / alternative approach**:\n\n* Separate class\n* Sorts **only by name**\n* Less flexible\n* Less cohesive\n\n### Comparison\n\n| Static Nested Comparator | External Comparator |\n| ------------------------ | ------------------- |\n| Inside `Employee`        | Separate file       |\n| Multiple strategies      | Single strategy     |\n| Better cohesion          | More scattered      |\n| Cleaner API              | More classes        |\n\n---\n\n## 7️⃣ Why generics `<T extends Employee>` here?\n\n👉 In this code, **it’s actually unnecessary**\n\nBecause:\n\n```java\nimplements Comparator<Employee>\n```\n\nThe generic `<T>` is not used anywhere.\n\n💡 **Learning takeaway**\nThis is common in learning code to introduce generics gradually.\n\n---\n\n## 8️⃣ Real-world analogy 🌍\n\nThink of `Employee` as:\n\n> **A product in an e-commerce app**\n\nSorting options:\n\n* Price\n* Name\n* Rating\n* Date added\n\nInstead of writing many comparator classes:\n\n```java\nEmployeeByNameComparator\nEmployeeByYearComparator\nEmployeeByIdComparator\n```\n\nYou use:\n\n```java\nnew Employee.EmployeeComparator<>(\"yearStarted\")\n```\n\n➡️ **Cleaner, scalable, professional design**\n\n---\n\n## 9️⃣ Key takeaways (Exam + Interview ready)\n\n✅ Static nested classes:\n\n* Belong logically to outer class\n* Do not need outer instance\n\n✅ Comparator:\n\n* Separates sorting logic from data\n* Supports chaining (`reversed`, `thenComparing`)\n\n✅ Design principle:\n\n> **High cohesion, low coupling**\n\n---\n\n## 10️⃣ If you want next…\n\nI can:\n\n* Rewrite this using **Lambda expressions**\n* Convert it to **Enum-based sorting** (BEST PRACTICE)\n* Show **Spring Boot / JPA real-world usage**\n* Explain **why Comparable is not used here**\n\nJust tell me 👍\n",
      "metadata": {
